# Parallel Computing with Wolfram Language

A practical introduction to using Wolfram Language parallel kernels for independent work, data-parallel mapping, shared definitions, and simple performance checks.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Parallel Computing with Wolfram Language

A practical introduction to using Wolfram Language parallel kernels for independent work, data-parallel mapping, shared definitions, and simple performance checks.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 1. Start parallel kernels

Parallel computations run on separate Wolfram Language kernels. `LaunchKernels[]` starts the configured local subkernels, and `ParallelEvaluate` lets us ask each subkernel what it is.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 1. Start parallel kernels

Parallel computations run on separate Wolfram Language kernels. `LaunchKernels[]` starts the configured local subkernels, and `ParallelEvaluate` lets us ask each subkernel what it is.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
LaunchKernels[];

kernelSummary = ParallelEvaluate[
  <|
    "KernelID" -> $KernelID,
    "Machine" -> $MachineName,
    "ProcessID" -> $ProcessID
  |>
];

Dataset[kernelSummary]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
