.md
## 2. Data parallelism with `ParallelTable`

The best first use case is a list of independent calculations. Each item can be computed without looking at the others, so Wolfram Language can spread the work across kernels.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 2. Data parallelism with `ParallelTable`

The best first use case is a list of independent calculations. Each item can be computed without looking at the others, so Wolfram Language can spread the work across kernels.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Clear[primeBlockTotal];
primeBlockTotal[start_Integer] := Total[Prime /@ Range[start, start + 1499]];
DistributeDefinitions[primeBlockTotal];

starts = Range[10000, 30000, 2500];

serialTiming = First @ AbsoluteTiming[
  serialResult = Table[primeBlockTotal[start], {start, starts}]
];

parallelTiming = First @ AbsoluteTiming[
  parallelResult = ParallelTable[
    primeBlockTotal[start],
    {start, starts},
    Method -> "CoarsestGrained"
  ]
];

<|
  "SameResults" -> serialResult === parallelResult,
  "SerialSeconds" -> serialTiming,
  "ParallelSeconds" -> parallelTiming,
  "ObservedSpeedup" -> serialTiming/parallelTiming
|>//TableView 

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["17958ab3-1043-4299-aa99-3183b99ecf3b"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKG5pbmlokJhnrGhqYGOuaGFla6iYmAgljQwvjJEvL1OQ04yQAcYoVIQ=="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Small examples do not always become faster. Parallel kernels have startup, scheduling, and data-transfer overhead. Parallelism is most useful when each item is expensive enough to pay for that overhead.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Small examples do not always become faster. Parallel kernels have startup, scheduling, and data-transfer overhead. Parallelism is most useful when each item is expensive enough to pay for that overhead.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 3. Mapping work with `ParallelMap`

`ParallelMap[f, list]` is the parallel version of `Map[f, list]`. It is useful when a function should be applied independently to every element.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 3. Mapping work with `ParallelMap`

`ParallelMap[f, list]` is the parallel version of `Map[f, list]`. It is useful when a function should be applied independently to every element.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Clear[textFeatures];
textFeatures[text_String] := <|
  "Text" -> text,
  "Characters" -> StringLength[text],
  "Words" -> WordCount[text],
  "Vowels" -> StringCount[ToLowerCase[text], Alternatives @@ Characters["aeiou"]]
|>;
DistributeDefinitions[textFeatures];

sentences = {
  "Parallel kernels evaluate independent tasks.",
  "Definitions must be available on every subkernel.",
  "Large tasks usually scale better than tiny tasks.",
  "Return values are easier to reason about than shared mutation."
};

TableView @ ParallelMap[textFeatures, sentences]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["49989195-4154-48e1-b2b0-4e22333daeb5"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKm1haWlgaWprqmhiamuiaWKQa6iYZJRnomqQaGRkbG6ckpiaZAgBrtRTm"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 4. Send definitions to subkernels

Subkernels do not automatically know every definition created in the main kernel. Use `DistributeDefinitions` before calling custom functions in parallel.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 4. Send definitions to subkernels

Subkernels do not automatically know every definition created in the main kernel. Use `DistributeDefinitions` before calling custom functions in parallel.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Clear[estimatePi];
estimatePi[samples_Integer] := Module[{points, inside},
  points = RandomReal[{-1, 1}, {samples, 2}];
  inside = Count[Total[points^2, {2}], x_ /; x <= 1];
  N[4 inside/samples]
];

DistributeDefinitions[estimatePi];

estimates = ParallelTable[estimatePi[75000], {8}];

<|
  "Estimates" -> estimates,
  "MeanEstimate" -> Mean[estimates],
  "Error" -> Abs[Mean[estimates] - Pi]
|>//TableView  

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["e589a688-36bf-47e9-ba57-0a90d488879b"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKp5paWCaaWVjoGpslpemamKda6iYlmprrGiRaGqSYWFhYmFsmAQCB5BVq"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 5. Run setup on every kernel

`ParallelEvaluate` is also useful for per-kernel initialization. Here each subkernel receives a deterministic random seed based on its own `$KernelID`.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 5. Run setup on every kernel

`ParallelEvaluate` is also useful for per-kernel initialization. Here each subkernel receives a deterministic random seed based on its own `$KernelID`.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ParallelEvaluate[SeedRandom[2026 + $KernelID]];

ParallelTable[
  <|"Task" -> task, "KernelID" -> $KernelID, "RandomValue" -> RandomReal[]|>,
  {task, 8}
] // TableView

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["804b4d1b-a0ad-4a2f-9933-5c5e36c4cc7e"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKWxiYJJmkGCbpJhokpuiaJBql6VpaGhvrmiabphqbJZskJ5unAgCHYRX7"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 6. A compact pattern for real work

The most reliable pattern is:

1. Start kernels with `LaunchKernels[]`.
2. Define ordinary Wolfram Language functions in the master kernel.
3. Send needed definitions with `DistributeDefinitions`.
4. Use `ParallelMap`, `ParallelTable`, `ParallelDo`, or related parallel functions.
5. Return values instead of relying on shared mutation when possible.
6. Close kernels when the notebook is finished.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 6. A compact pattern for real work

The most reliable pattern is:

1. Start kernels with `LaunchKernels[]`.
2. Define ordinary Wolfram Language functions in the master kernel.
3. Send needed definitions with `DistributeDefinitions`.
4. Use `ParallelMap`, `ParallelTable`, `ParallelDo`, or related parallel functions.
5. Return values instead of relying on shared mutation when possible.
6. Close kernels when the notebook is finished.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Clear[expensiveScore];
expensiveScore[n_Integer] := Module[{sample},
  Pause[0.05];
  sample = Prime /@ Range[n, n + 999];
  <|
    "n" -> n,
    "KernelID" -> $KernelID,
    "PrimeSum" -> Total[sample],
    "PrimeMean" -> N[Mean[sample]]
  |>
];

DistributeDefinitions[expensiveScore];

Dataset @ ParallelMap[expensiveScore, Range[2000, 2007], Method -> "CoarsestGrained"]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["1ee36dcb-ad48-486a-8e48-b23e2dea055a"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKG6amGpulJCfpJqaYWOiaWJgl6lqkAllJRsapRimpiQampokAkUQWMA=="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 7. Asynchronous parallel submission

WLJS also provides `ParallelSubmitAsync[expr]`, which submits work to an available parallel kernel and immediately returns a `Promise`. This is useful when a master kernel should stay responsive while the parallel kernel works.

For tutorial cells we can call `WaitAll[promise, seconds]` to show a synchronous result, but interactive notebooks usually attach a callback with `Then[promise, callback]` instead.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 7. Asynchronous parallel submission

WLJS also provides `ParallelSubmitAsync[expr]`, which submits work to an available parallel kernel and immediately returns a `Promise`. This is useful when a master kernel should stay responsive while the parallel kernel works.

For tutorial cells we can call `WaitAll[promise, seconds]` to show a synchronous result, but interactive notebooks usually attach a callback with `Then[promise, callback]` instead.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
asyncPromise = ParallelSubmitAsync[
  Pause[0.75];
  <|
    "KernelID" -> $KernelID,
    "FinishedAt" -> DateString[{"Hour", ":", "Minute", ":", "Second"}],
    "Result" -> Total[Range[100000]]
  |>
];

<|
  "ReturnedImmediatelyAs" -> Head[asyncPromise],
  "SynchronousResultForTutorial" -> WaitAll[asyncPromise, 5]
|> // TableView

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["b775f13d-085f-4e87-a4c1-f8522818222e"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKJ5mbm6YZGqfoGliYpumapFqY6yaaJBvqplmYGhlZGFoYGRmlAgB/eRUd"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
The promise can be subscribed to with `Then`. The callback runs when the parallel result is ready. In this example the callback stores a compact summary in `asyncCallbackResult`; a short `Pause` at the end only gives the callback time to update the displayed value.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
The promise can be subscribed to with `Then`. The callback runs when the parallel result is ready. In this example the callback stores a compact summary in `asyncCallbackResult`; a short `Pause` at the end only gives the callback time to update the displayed value.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
asyncCallbackResult = Missing["Waiting"];

callbackPromise = ParallelSubmitAsync[
  Pause[0.5];
  <|"KernelID" -> $KernelID, "Value" -> 144|>
];

Then[
  callbackPromise,
  Function[result,
    asyncCallbackResult = <|
      "Handled" -> True,
      "OriginalResult" -> result,
      "DoubledValue" -> 2 result["Value"]
    |>;
    Null
  ]
];

Pause[1];
asyncCallbackResult

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
<|"Handled"->True,"OriginalResult"-><|"KernelID"->9,"Value"->144|>,"DoubledValue"->288|>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Multiple async jobs can be submitted first and collected later. The important point is that submission is non-blocking; the waits below happen after all promises have already been created.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Multiple async jobs can be submitted first and collected later. The important point is that submission is non-blocking; the waits below happen after all promises have already been created.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Clear[asyncScore];
asyncScore[n_Integer] := Module[{},
  Pause[0.25];
  <|
    "n" -> n,
    "KernelID" -> $KernelID,
    "Score" -> Total[Range[n]^2]
  |>
];

DistributeDefinitions[asyncScore];

asyncPromises = Table[
  With[{task = n}, ParallelSubmitAsync[asyncScore[task]]],
  {n, 5, 10}
];

Dataset[WaitAll[#, 5] &/@ asyncPromises]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["123a8afa-0f3b-47d9-acd3-cb7f0c5eac4b"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKGxoZJ1okpiXqGqQZJ+mamKdY6iYmpxjrJieZpxkkm6YmJpskAQCQuhax"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
